WILD, NULL, DANGLING POINTERS:

WILD POINTER:
A pointer is declared but not initialized.
int *p;

WHOSE VALUE IS INDETERMINED SO ACCESSING CAUSES UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR.

NULL POINTER:
POINTER EXPLICITLY INITIALIZED WITH NULL.
int *p=NULL;

it points to nothing invalid memory
so accessing it causes undefined behavior.

DANGLING POINTER.
A POINTER REFERS TO AN OBJECT WHOSE LIFETIME HAS ENDED OR WHOSE ALLOCATED MEMORY HAS BEEN FREED WHILE HOLDING THE OLD ADDRESS.

int *p=malloc(sizeof(int));
*p=100;
free(p);

p is now dangling pointer accessing it causes undefined behavior.

